You are the planning brain for a multi-task GSAP integration project on Lucifer's Diary (a Next.js literary erotica site, currently on develop branch).

INPUTS FROM RESEARCH PHASE:

DOCK DEMO: {"url":"https://demos.gsap.com/demo/macos-dock-effect/","title":"macOS Dock Effect","technique":"FETCH FAILED: WebFetch could not retrieve the actual source. The demos.gsap.com page is a thin wrapper that loads the demo via an iframe pointing at CodePen (https://codepen.io/GreenSock/pen/PwNeKZy), and CodePen returns HTTP 403 to WebFetch on every variant tried (pen page, /fullpage, .html, .js). The wrapper page itself contains only metadata and nav — no inline source. So the description below is the canonical mechanic from knowledge, not verbatim from the demo. Canonical macOS-dock magnification mechanic: a single mousemove listener is attached to the dock container (NOT per-card — one listener, then loop over cards each move). On every move event, iterate the cards and for each card compute the horizontal distance between the cursor's clientX and the card's center (rect.left + rect.width/2). Convert that distance into a 0..1 falloff using a fixed radius of influence (e.g. 200px): t = Math.max(0, 1 - distance / radius). Map t to a target scale, e.g. scale = 1 + t * 0.8 (so the hovered card hits ~1.8, neighbours interpolate down to 1). Optionally lift the card with a small y offset (y = -t * 20) so the magnified items rise like the real dock. Push each card's properties via gsap.to(card, { scale, y, duration: 0.3, ease: 'power2.out', overwrite: 'auto' }) — overwrite:'auto' is critical so the previous tween on the same card is killed when a new mousemove fires, otherwise tweens queue and lag. On mouseleave of the container, gsap.to all cards back to { scale: 1, y: 0, duration: 0.5, ease: 'power3.out' }. Performance hardening usually seen in the GSAP demo: cache the card rects once on load and on resize (rather than calling getBoundingClientRect each move), set transform-origin: bottom center on the cards so they grow upward like real dock icons, give the container display:flex with align-items:flex-end so siblings reflow around the scaled item, and use gsap.quickTo(card, 'scale') / gsap.quickTo(card, 'y') as pre-baked setters for max throughput (quickTo skips tween-creation overhead on every move). No special plugin is needed — core gsap + CSS transforms is enough; the GSAP team's pen does NOT register Flip, MotionPath, or Draggable for this effect.","keyMechanic":"Single mousemove listener on the dock container, loop over cards, distance = |cursorX - cardCenterX|, falloff t = max(0, 1 - distance/radius), then gsap.to(card, { scale: 1 + t*0.8, y: -t*20, ease: 'power2.out', overwrite: 'auto' }). On mouseleave, tween all cards back to scale 1.","gsapPlugins":["gsap-core"],"isPaidPlugin":false,"fetchSuccess":false,"codeSnippet":"// Canonical reconstruction — NOT verbatim from the GSAP demo (CodePen returned 403)
const dock = document.querySelector('.dock');
const cards = gsap.utils.toArray('.dock .card');

const RADIUS = 200;        // px of influence on either side of cursor
const MAX_SCALE = 1.8;     // peak magnification at distance 0
const MAX_LIFT = 20;       // px the icon rises at distance 0

// Pre-bake high-throughput setters so we don't allocate a tween per move event
const setters = cards.map(card => ({
  scale: gsap.quickTo(card, 'scale', { duration: 0.3, ease: 'power2.out' }),
  y:     gsap.quickTo(card, 'y',     { duration: 0.3, ease: 'power2.out' }),
}));

// Cache card centers; refresh on resize so distance math stays correct
let centers = [];
const measure = () => {
  centers = cards.map(card => {
    const r = card.getBoundingClientRect();
    return r.left + r.width / 2;
  });
};
measure();
window.addEventListener('resize', measure);

dock.addEventListener('mousemove', (e) => {
  const x = e.clientX;
  for (let i = 0; i < cards.length; i++) {
    const dist = Math.abs(x - centers[i]);
    const t = Math.max(0, 1 - dist / RADIUS);     // 0..1 falloff
    setters[i].scale(1 + t * (MAX_SCALE - 1));
    setters[i].y(-t * MAX_LIFT);
  }
});

dock.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
  cards.forEach(card => {
    gsap.to(card, { scale: 1, y: 0, duration: 0.5, ease: 'power3.out', overwrite: 'auto' });
  });
});

/* CSS essentials
.dock { display: flex; align-items: flex-end; gap: 12px; padding: 12px; }
.dock .card { transform-origin: bottom center; will-change: transform; }
*/","reactPort":"Port to React/Next.js with @gsap/react's useGSAP hook. Mark the component 'use client' in Next.js (App Router). Refs: a dockRef on the container, and cards collected via gsap.utils.toArray(dockRef.current.querySelectorAll('.card')) inside useGSAP. useGSAP runs once on mount, scoped to dockRef, and returns a context whose revert() auto-kills tweens + removes inline transforms on unmount — so no manual cleanup of the listeners or quickTo setters is needed beyond removing the DOM listeners (do that in the contextSafe return). Sketch: import { useGSAP } from '@gsap/react'; import gsap from 'gsap'; const ref = useRef(null); useGSAP(() => { const cards = gsap.utils.toArray('.card', ref.current); const setters = cards.map(c => ({ s: gsap.quickTo(c,'scale',{duration:.3,ease:'power2.out'}), y: gsap.quickTo(c,'y',{duration:.3,ease:'power2.out'}) })); let centers = cards.map(c => { const r=c.getBoundingClientRect(); return r.left+r.width/2; }); const onMove = (e) => { for (let i=0;i<cards.length;i++){ const t=Math.max(0,1-Math.abs(e.clientX-centers[i])/200); setters[i].s(1+t*0.8); setters[i].y(-t*20); } }; const onLeave = () => cards.forEach(c => gsap.to(c,{scale:1,y:0,duration:.5,ease:'power3.out',overwrite:'auto'})); const onResize = () => { centers = cards.map(c => { const r=c.getBoundingClientRect(); return r.left+r.width/2; }); }; ref.current.addEventListener('mousemove', onMove); ref.current.addEventListener('mouseleave', onLeave); window.addEventListener('resize', onResize); return () => { ref.current?.removeEventListener('mousemove', onMove); ref.current?.removeEventListener('mouseleave', onLeave); window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize); }; }, { scope: ref }); JSX: <div ref={ref} className='dock'>{items.map(it => <div key={it.id} className='card'>{it.label}</div>)}</div>. Gotchas: (1) install @gsap/react and register useGSAP — no gsap.registerPlugin needed for core. (2) In React 18 StrictMode useGSAP handles the double-mount cleanly because of context.revert(). (3) Don't put the mousemove listener in a plain useEffect that re-runs on every state change — keep it inside useGSAP so refs and setters are stable. (4) For touch devices, gate the listener behind a matchMedia('(hover: hover)') check or fall back to a tap-to-magnify variant.","a11yNotes":"Keyboard users get nothing from a cursor-proximity effect — pair it with a :focus-visible style on .card that mimics the magnified state (transform: scale(1.4) translateY(-12px)) so tab navigation still surfaces which item is active. Wrap the listener attach in matchMedia('(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)') so touch users don't get a stuck magnification on tap; alternatively respect prefers-reduced-motion by short-circuiting the mousemove handler (or dropping duration to 0) when window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches. If the cards are icons-only, every .card needs an aria-label or visible text; if it's a nav, wrap in <nav> with a role and put the items in a <ul>. Don't rely on color alone for the active/hover state — the scale change itself is the affordance, which is fine for sighted users but invisible to screen readers, so also toggle aria-current or a visually-hidden announcement when the user actually activates an item."}

AUTO-HEIGHT DEMO: {"url":"https://demos.gsap.com/demo/animate-auto-height/","title":"Animate auto height (GSAP demo)","technique":"Measure-then-tween. CSS cannot transition `height: auto`, so the canonical GSAP recipe is: (1) read the natural pixel height of the content with `el.scrollHeight` (or `getBoundingClientRect().height` after temporarily setting `height: auto`), (2) tween from `height: 0` (or any starting px value) to that measured pixel value, (3) on completion, set `height: "auto"` so the element continues to respond to content changes after the animation. The Flip plugin can do the same thing more declaratively (`Flip.getState` -> change to `auto` -> `Flip.from(state, {...})`), but the lightweight measure-then-tween approach does NOT require Flip and is what the official GSAP "animate auto height" demo is built around.","keyMechanic":"The trick is that GSAP can tween any numeric value, so you simply convert "auto" into a concrete pixel target by reading `scrollHeight` before the tween starts, then animate to that number. The crucial final step is `onComplete: () => gsap.set(el, { height: "auto" })` — without it, the panel stays locked at the measured pixel height and breaks if the content later reflows (e.g. viewport resize, font load, nested expand). For a two-way open/close you typically build a paused timeline so you can `.play()` / `.reverse()` and you re-measure on every open in case content changed.","gsapPlugins":["gsap (core)"],"isPaidPlugin":false,"fetchSuccess":false,"codeSnippet":"// Canonical GSAP "animate to height: auto" pattern
// (WebFetch was blocked by Cloudflare on both demos.gsap.com and codepen.io;
//  this is the well-established recipe Jack Doyle/GreenSock recommends.)

import { gsap } from "gsap";

// One-shot open: 0 -> auto
function openPanel(el) {
  gsap.set(el, { height: 0, overflow: "hidden", display: "block" });
  gsap.to(el, {
    height: el.scrollHeight, // measured pixel target
    duration: 0.5,
    ease: "power2.inOut",
    onComplete: () => {
      // Release the lock so the panel reflows naturally afterward.
      gsap.set(el, { height: "auto", overflow: "" });
    },
  });
}

// Reusable open/close helper (re-measures every time, handles re-entrance)
function animateHeight(el, open, { duration = 0.5, ease = "power2.inOut" } = {}) {
  // Kill any in-flight tween so we don't fight it
  gsap.killTweensOf(el);

  if (open) {
    // Snapshot current height, force auto to measure target, then tween back
    const start = el.getBoundingClientRect().height;
    gsap.set(el, { height: "auto", overflow: "hidden" });
    const end = el.getBoundingClientRect().height;
    gsap.fromTo(
      el,
      { height: start },
      {
        height: end,
        duration,
        ease,
        onComplete: () => gsap.set(el, { height: "auto" }),
      }
    );
  } else {
    const start = el.getBoundingClientRect().height;
    gsap.fromTo(
      el,
      { height: start },
      { height: 0, overflow: "hidden", duration, ease }
    );
  }
}","reactPort":"// Next.js / React port using @gsap/react's useGSAP
// File: app/components/AutoHeightPanel.tsx  (or pages/... in pages router)
"use client";

import { useRef, useState } from "react";
import { gsap } from "gsap";
import { useGSAP } from "@gsap/react";

type Props = { children: React.ReactNode; open: boolean };

export function AutoHeightPanel({ children, open }: Props) {
  const panelRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  // Hooked into `open` so it re-runs whenever the prop flips.
  // useGSAP handles cleanup (kills tweens) on unmount + before re-runs.
  useGSAP(
    () => {
      const el = panelRef.current;
      if (!el) return;

      // Measure target by briefly forcing height: auto
      const startH = el.getBoundingClientRect().height;
      gsap.set(el, { height: "auto", overflow: "hidden" });
      const fullH = el.getBoundingClientRect().height;

      gsap.fromTo(
        el,
        { height: startH },
        {
          height: open ? fullH : 0,
          duration: 0.5,
          ease: "power2.inOut",
          onComplete: () => {
            // Only release the lock when fully open; keep height:0 when closed
            if (open) gsap.set(el, { height: "auto" });
          },
        }
      );
    },
    { dependencies: [open], scope: panelRef }
  );

  return (
    <div
      ref={panelRef}
      style={{
        height: 0,           // start collapsed; SSR-safe
        overflow: "hidden",
      }}
      aria-hidden={!open}
    >
      {children}
    </div>
  );
}

// Usage:
// const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
// <button onClick={() => setOpen(o => !o)}>Toggle</button>
// <AutoHeightPanel open={open}><p>Any dynamic content here…</p></AutoHeightPanel>

// Notes for Next.js:
//  - Mark the component "use client" — useGSAP/refs need the client runtime.
//  - Install: `npm i gsap @gsap/react`. No paid/Club plugins required.
//  - If content can change while open (e.g. async data), re-run by adding
//    a `key` or another dependency to useGSAP so it re-measures.","a11yNotes":"Set `overflow: hidden` only during the tween — leaving it on can clip focus rings and tooltips. Add `aria-hidden={!open}` (or better, also toggle `inert` on the collapsed panel) so screen readers and keyboard tab order skip hidden content. Pair the trigger with `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` pointing at the panel's id. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: wrap the tween in `gsap.matchMedia()` and use `duration: 0` (or a `gsap.set`) when the user prefers reduced motion. Avoid animating height on elements that contain focusable inputs unless you also manage focus, otherwise focus can land in a 0-height region."}

GSAP DOCS SUMMARY: {"coreAPI":["gsap.to(targets, vars) - Tweens from current state to vars values. targets: string|Element|Element[]|object|object[]; vars: object with properties to animate plus special props (duration, ease, delay, onComplete, etc). Returns a Tween. Example: gsap.to('.box', { rotation: 27, x: 100, duration: 1 })","gsap.from(targets, vars) - Tweens from vars values to current state. Same signature shape as to(). Useful for 'reveal' animations where the end state is the rendered state. Returns Tween.","gsap.fromTo(targets, fromVars, toVars) - Explicit start and end values. fromVars defines initial property values; toVars defines destination values plus special props (ease, duration, delay, onComplete). Returns Tween.","gsap.set(targets, vars) - Immediately sets properties (zero-duration tween). Useful for initial state setup. Returns Tween.","gsap.timeline(vars?) - Creates a Timeline sequencing container. vars accepts callbacks (onComplete, onStart, onUpdate, onRepeat, onReverseComplete + *Params variants), delay, duration, repeat, repeatDelay, yoyo, paused, defaults (inherited tween defaults), callbackScope, smoothChildTiming, autoRemoveChildren. Chainable methods: tl.to(target, vars, position), .from(...), .fromTo(target, fromVars, toVars, position), .set(target, vars, position), .add(child, position), .call(callback, params, position). The 'position' parameter accepts absolute time, relative ('+=0.5', '-=0.2'), or label strings.","gsap.registerPlugin(...plugins) - Variadic; pass any number of plugin objects. Required to prevent tree-shaking from dropping plugins in modern bundlers. Re-registering is a no-op (harmless). Example: gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger, SplitText, DrawSVGPlugin)."],"pluginRegistration":"In a Next.js project, gsap.registerPlugin() must run only on the client because plugins like ScrollTrigger reach for window/document at module-evaluation time. Practical pattern: keep registration inside a 'use client' file, ideally inside the component body (or inside the useGSAP callback), or guard with `if (typeof window !== 'undefined')`. A common pattern is a central `lib/gsap.ts` (marked 'use client') that imports gsap + plugins and calls gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger, SplitText, ...) once; other client components import from that file. Per the docs: 'you must explicitly register the plugin(s)' so bundler tree-shaking does not drop them, and registering multiple times is 'perfectly fine'.","freePluginsRelevant":["ScrollTrigger - always free","ScrollToPlugin - always free","Observer - always free","Flip - always free","Draggable - always free","TextPlugin - always free","EaselPlugin - always free","PixiPlugin - always free","MotionPathPlugin - FREE (previously bundled with Club, now fully free post-Webflow)","CustomEase - FREE as of the post-Webflow free release (formerly Club GSAP)","CustomBounce - FREE (formerly Club GSAP)","CustomWiggle - FREE (formerly Club GSAP)","SplitText - FREE (formerly Club GSAP / 'members-only', explicitly called out in the licensing page as now free)","MorphSVGPlugin - FREE (formerly Club GSAP, explicitly called out as now free)","DrawSVGPlugin - FREE (formerly Club GSAP)","InertiaPlugin - FREE (formerly Club GSAP, replaced ThrowPropsPlugin)","Physics2DPlugin - FREE (formerly Club GSAP)","PhysicsPropsPlugin - FREE (formerly Club GSAP)","MotionPathHelper - FREE (formerly Club GSAP)","GSDevTools - FREE (formerly Club GSAP)","ScrollSmoother - FREE (formerly Club GSAP)"],"paidPluginsToAvoid":[],"useGSAPHookPattern":"Import: `import { useGSAP } from '@gsap/react'` and optionally `gsap.registerPlugin(useGSAP)` to avoid React version mismatches. Signature: `useGSAP(callback, configOrDeps?)`. The callback receives `(context, contextSafe)` and runs inside a gsap.context() so all animations/ScrollTriggers/Draggables/SplitText created during it are tracked. Scope ref: pass `{ scope: containerRef }` so every selector inside the callback (e.g. '.box') is automatically scoped to descendants of containerRef.current — no manual querying needed. Deps: pass a `dependencies` array (or an array as the 2nd arg) just like useEffect; combine with `revertOnUpdate: true` to revert on every dep change (defaults to reverting only on unmount). contextSafe: wrap any handler created AFTER the hook runs (click handlers, setTimeout callbacks) so the animations they create are still tracked and cleaned up — either via the second callback arg `(ctx, contextSafe) => { const onClick = contextSafe(() => gsap.to(...)) }` or via the destructured return `const { contextSafe } = useGSAP({ scope: ref })`. Return cleanup: return a function from the callback to remove event listeners or other side effects — GSAP animations themselves are reverted automatically, so the returned cleanup is only for non-GSAP teardown (e.g. `return () => el.removeEventListener('click', handler)`). Canonical pattern: ```const container = useRef(null); useGSAP(() => { gsap.to('.box', { rotation: 180, duration: 1 }); }, { scope: container });``` then `<div ref={container}><div className='box' /></div>`.","nextjsClientComponentGuidance":"Per GSAP docs: 'If you're using the app router / react server components, you need to drop a use client at the top of your file for useGSAP() to work.' Concretely: (1) Mark any file that imports `gsap`, any plugin, or `useGSAP` with `'use client'` at the top — these modules touch window/document at evaluation and cannot run on the server. (2) Call `gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger, SplitText, ...)` only on the client; safest place is inside the component body or inside the useGSAP callback. A central client-only `lib/gsap.ts` that registers once is a clean pattern. (3) useGSAP uses an isomorphic layout effect internally — it prefers useLayoutEffect but falls back to useEffect when window is undefined, so the hook itself is SSR-safe to import, but its callback only fires on the client. (4) Cleanup is automatic when the scope ref unmounts: the underlying gsap.context() reverts every tween, ScrollTrigger, Draggable, and SplitText created during the hook. You only need to return a cleanup function for non-GSAP side effects (event listeners, observers). (5) Never call gsap.to/timeline/etc at module top-level in a server component — wrap in useGSAP or a useEffect inside a 'use client' component.","licenseAsOfToday":"As of today (2026-06-09), per gsap.com/licensing (effective April 30, 2025): GSAP is 'free for everyone' thanks to Webflow's support. Verbatim from the page: 'Can I really use GSAP in commercial projects without paying anything? Yes, really!' and 'All of GSAP including the plugins that were formerly members-only like SplitText and MorphSVG can be used in commercial projects at no charge.' Commercial usage is covered under the standard license. The only restriction in the Prohibited Uses section is against building competing visual animation builders. Club GSAP is no longer a paywall for plugins — there is no premium plugin tier to avoid."}

NEXT.JS STARTERS: {"starters":[{"name":"GSAP NextJS Starter","url":"https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-3zkguu","description":"Bare-bones GSAP-in-Next.js scaffold: a single component running a basic gsap.to/timeline, nothing else.","relevantFor":"(c) button click feedback — smallest possible base for wiring gsap.to into onClick. Also the cleanest starting canvas for (a) the macOS-dock hover demo."},{"name":"GSAP ScrollTrigger NextJS Starter (forked)","url":"https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-u6rgzq","description":"Minimal Next.js + ScrollTrigger setup showing registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger) and a scroll-driven timeline (Pages Router flavor).","relevantFor":"(e) animated filter/reorder — useful as the registerPlugin reference when you add Flip alongside ScrollTrigger; also a backup base for (a) if dock entrance should be scroll-triggered."},{"name":"GSAP ScrollTrigger App Router","url":"https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-hskbf2","description":"The App Router version of the ScrollTrigger starter: 'use client' + useGSAP + module-scope registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger).","relevantFor":"(a) macOS-dock hover on cards — best modern App Router base to drop a dock component into. Also the canonical client-component shape for (b) and (e)."},{"name":"GSAP ScrollSmoother NextJS Starter","url":"https://stackblitz.com/edit/nextjs-efumgr","description":"ScrollSmoother wrapper setup on the (older) Pages Router, showing the smooth-wrapper + smooth-content markup and ScrollSmoother.create().","relevantFor":"(d) auto-scroll to saved position — demonstrates the ScrollSmoother instance you'd call .scrollTo(savedY) on (Pages Router variant)."},{"name":"GSAP ScrollSmoother NextJS App Folder","url":"https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-lq58ww","description":"Same ScrollSmoother demo migrated to the experimental Next.js app/ folder, illustrating the 'use client' boundary around the smooth wrapper.","relevantFor":"(d) auto-scroll to saved position — App Router variant of the ScrollSmoother setup; good reference for where to put 'use client' relative to the smoother wrapper."},{"name":"ScrollSmoother & useGSAP with App Router (forked)","url":"https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-cxedmc","description":"The canonical 'do it the right way today' demo: App Router + 'use client' + useGSAP() + ScrollSmoother with scoped refs and automatic cleanup.","relevantFor":"(d) auto-scroll to saved position — the exact pattern to copy (ScrollSmoother.scrollTo from inside useGSAP). Also the best reference scaffold for (b) animating auto-height with useGSAP + scoped refs."},{"name":"GSAP Page Transitions Next13","url":"https://stackblitz.com/edit/nextjs-13cw4u","description":"Route-change page transitions in Next 13 driven by GSAP timelines coordinated with React Transition Group's enter/exit lifecycle.","relevantFor":"(e) animated filter/reorder — closest reference for FLIP-style choreography of items entering/leaving (the enter/exit timeline pattern transfers directly to filter reorders, even though Flip itself isn't used here)."},{"name":"Overall pattern across the collection","url":"https://stackblitz.com/@GSAP-dev/collections/gsap-nextjs-starters","description":"useGSAP is the preferred entry point in every App Router starter, called as useGSAP(() => {...}, { scope: containerRef }) with automatic cleanup (no manual gsap.context().revert() needed). gsap.registerPlugin(useGSAP, ScrollTrigger, ScrollSmoother, ...) is always called ONCE at module scope, right after imports — never inside the hook, never inside useEffect. 'use client' is required at the top of any file touching gsap/useGSAP/ScrollTrigger/ScrollSmoother under the App Router (starters 3, 5, 6, 7); Pages-Router starters (1, 2, 4) don't need it because pages are already client components. App Router vs Pages Router split: titles/slugs say it — 'App Router'/'App Folder' starters (#3, #5, #6) and newer stackblitz-starters-* slugs are App Router; nextjs-efumgr (#4) and nextjs-13cw4u (#7) are Pages Router. GSAP's current recommendation, embodied by starter #6, is: App Router + client component + module-scope registerPlugin + useGSAP with a scoped ref.","relevantFor":"All effects (a)-(e) should follow this shape: a 'use client' file, registerPlugin at module top, useGSAP(() => {...}, { scope: ref }) inside the component."}]}

BUTTON CLICK REC: {"useCase":"Click feedback for LIKE / SAVE / SHARE buttons in Lucifer's Diary reading view. The animation must feel "alive" but stay short (~450ms) so rapid taps don't queue up. Color must be customizable per button via a CSS variable (--button-color: like-red, gold, neutral), and prefers-reduced-motion users get an instant, no-motion state change instead of the bounce.","plugin":"gsap core only — gsap.timeline() with elastic.out and back.out easings. No paid plugins (no Physics2D, no MotionPath). The React integration uses @gsap/react's useGSAP + contextSafe so the click handler is GC-safe and auto-reverted on unmount. Reduced-motion is gated with window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)") — simpler and more local than spinning up gsap.matchMedia for a one-off micro-interaction.","approach":"A 3-step timeline that reads like a real tactile "press": (1) a fast 80ms scale-down to 0.85 on power2.out — the "press" — (2) an elastic snap back to 1 over ~420ms with back.out(2.4), and (3) a parallel 250ms color flash where the icon's currentColor lifts from neutral to the button's --button-color and settles back, using gsap.fromTo on the CSS variable itself so per-button theming is genuinely one line in CSS. The whole timeline is constructed inside a contextSafe handler so React re-renders never leak tweens, and it kills any in-flight tween on the same element before starting so spam-clicks restart cleanly instead of stacking. Reduced-motion short-circuits: we set the final visual state (active class / aria-pressed) and skip the timeline entirely — the state still toggles, just without movement. The same component handles all three buttons; only the CSS variable and the icon swap.","codeSketch":"// ReactionButton.tsx
import { useRef } from "react";
import gsap from "gsap";
import { useGSAP } from "@gsap/react";

gsap.registerPlugin(useGSAP);

export function ReactionButton({ icon, color, onToggle, active, label }) {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);

  const { contextSafe } = useGSAP({ scope: ref });

  const handleClick = contextSafe(() => {
    onToggle?.();
    const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
    if (reduce) return; // state already toggled; skip motion

    const el = ref.current!;
    const iconEl = el.querySelector(".rx-icon");
    gsap.killTweensOf([el, iconEl]); // restart cleanly on spam clicks

    gsap.timeline({ defaults: { overwrite: "auto" } })
      .to(el, { scale: 0.85, duration: 0.08, ease: "power2.out" })
      .to(el, { scale: 1,    duration: 0.42, ease: "back.out(2.4)" })
      .fromTo(iconEl,
        { "--flash": 0 },
        { "--flash": 1, duration: 0.18, ease: "power1.out" }, 0)
      .to(iconEl, { "--flash": 0, duration: 0.25, ease: "power1.in" }, 0.18);
  });

  return (
    <button ref={ref} onClick={handleClick} aria-pressed={active} aria-label={label}
            className="rx-btn" style={{ "--button-color": color } as any}>
      <span className="rx-icon">{icon}</span>
    </button>
  );
}

/* CSS — per-button color is one line at the call site via the `color` prop.
.rx-btn   { background: transparent; border: 0; cursor: pointer; will-change: transform; }
.rx-icon  { color: color-mix(in oklab, currentColor, var(--button-color) calc(var(--flash, 0) * 100%)); display: inline-flex; }
*/

// Usage:
// <ReactionButton color="var(--like-red)"  icon={<HeartIcon/>}  ... />
// <ReactionButton color="var(--gold)"      icon={<BookmarkIcon/>} ... />
// <ReactionButton color="var(--gold-soft)" icon={<ShareIcon/>}  ... />","rationale":"No GSAP demo site URL turned out to be a perfect 1:1 match for "three small reading-view reaction buttons with per-button color theming." The closest direct reference is the Paradigma Digital tutorial on GSAP particle bursts for like buttons, but it requires the Physics2DPlugin (paid Club GreenSock) and a particle DOM the diary doesn't need. The canonical core-GSAP technique — short timeline + elastic.out for tactile press, with a parallel CSS-variable flash for color — is well established in the GSAP community pulse-button threads and is what I've used here. It also matches GreenSock's official guidance to wrap event-driven tweens in contextSafe (see the @gsap/react README) and to gate motion behind matchMedia for reduced-motion compliance.","recommendedDemo":"No single demos.gsap.com pen covers this exact pattern. The closest canonical references are: (a) GreenSock's official gsap.matchMedia reduced-motion pen at https://codepen.io/GreenSock/pen/RwMQwpR for the accessibility gate, and (b) the @gsap/react README at https://github.com/greensock/react for the useGSAP + contextSafe click-handler pattern. The Paradigma Digital tutorial (https://en.paradigmadigital.com/dev/how-use-gsap-create-particle-effects-dom/) is the best reference for a richer particle-burst variant if you later add Physics2D and want a full heart-burst on the LIKE button only.","a11yNotes":"Three accessibility guarantees baked into the sketch: (1) prefers-reduced-motion is checked on every click and the timeline is skipped entirely — the toggle state still flips, so no functionality is gated behind motion. (2) aria-pressed reflects the active state so screen readers announce LIKE/SAVE correctly as toggle buttons; SHARE should use aria-label only since it's an action, not a toggle. (3) The animation never exceeds ~500ms total and runs only on intentional click, so it can't trigger vestibular issues; back.out(2.4) overshoots only ~6%, well below seizure/discomfort thresholds. Hit targets stay at their resting size for layout — only the visual scale tweens — so the click area never shrinks under the pointer."}

SCROLL RESTORE REC: {"useCase":"A returning reader lands on `/chapter/[id]` (or `/story/[id]`). A hook (e.g. `useResumeScroll`) fetches their saved Y offset from `GET /api/reading-positions/:contentType/:contentId`. Once the article has rendered + hydrated, the page smoothly animates the window back down to that offset so the reader picks up exactly where they left off — without the jank of a hard `scrollTo(0, y)` snap, without fighting `TextFragmentScroller` (which handles `#:~:text=` deep-links), and without trapping the reader if they start scrolling themselves mid-animation.","plugin":"GSAP ScrollToPlugin (free, core distribution) — the canonical GSAP primitive for tweening `window.scrollY` (or any scrollable container) with an ease. Register it once, alongside the existing `ScrollTrigger` registration, in `frontend/lib/gsap.ts`. Drive it through the existing `@gsap/react` `useGSAP` hook so cleanup is automatic on unmount/route change.","approach":"Add `gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollToPlugin)` next to the existing `ScrollTrigger` registration in `frontend/lib/gsap.ts` (still SSR-guarded by the `typeof window !== 'undefined'` check). Create a new client component `ResumeScrollAnimator` (sibling of `TextFragmentScroller`, mounted on chapter + story pages) that reads the saved `y` from the existing `/api/reading-positions` hook, then — after a double `requestAnimationFrame` so RichText hydration has settled and the document height is final — fires `gsap.to(window, { duration: 1.2, ease: "power2.inOut", scrollTo: { y, autoKill: true, onAutoKill } })`. `autoKill: true` lets a wheel / touch / scrollbar grab cancel the tween instantly, so the reader is never trapped. Reduced-motion + deep-link coordination are handled before the tween is queued: if `prefersReducedMotion()` is true, fall back to a synchronous `window.scrollTo(0, y)`; if `window.location.hash.includes(":~:text=")` (or any non-empty hash), skip entirely and let `TextFragmentScroller` / the native fragment directive win — the two never race.","codeSketch":"// frontend/lib/gsap.ts — register once, SSR-safe
import { gsap } from 'gsap';
import { ScrollTrigger } from 'gsap/ScrollTrigger';
import { ScrollToPlugin } from 'gsap/ScrollToPlugin';
if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
  gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger, ScrollToPlugin);
}
export { gsap, ScrollTrigger };
export { useGSAP } from '@gsap/react';

// frontend/components/ResumeScrollAnimator.tsx
"use client";
import { useRef } from "react";
import { gsap, useGSAP, prefersReducedMotion } from "@/lib/gsap";
import { useSavedReadingPosition } from "@/lib/useSavedReadingPosition"; // wraps /api/reading-positions

interface Props { contentType: "chapter" | "story"; contentId: string; }

export default function ResumeScrollAnimator({ contentType, contentId }: Props) {
  const { y, ready } = useSavedReadingPosition(contentType, contentId); // null until fetched
  const scope = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  useGSAP(() => {
    if (!ready || y == null || y < 8) return;                 // nothing to restore
    if (typeof window === "undefined") return;                // SSR guard
    if (window.location.hash.includes(":~:text=")) return;    // let TextFragmentScroller win
    if (window.location.hash.length > 1) return;              // and any plain #anchor
    if (prefersReducedMotion()) { window.scrollTo(0, y); return; } // a11y fallback

    // Double rAF — article + RichText must hydrate so doc height is final,
    // otherwise `y` may exceed the current scrollMax and we land short.
    let raf1 = 0, raf2 = 0;
    raf1 = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      raf2 = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
        gsap.to(window, {
          duration: 1.2,
          ease: "power2.inOut",
          scrollTo: {
            y: Math.min(y, max),
            autoKill: true,            // user wheel/touch/scrollbar cancels instantly
            onAutoKill: () => { /* optional: analytics */ },
          },
        });
      });
    });
    return () => { cancelAnimationFrame(raf1); cancelAnimationFrame(raf2); };
  }, { scope, dependencies: [ready, y] });

  return <div ref={scope} hidden aria-hidden />;
}","rationale":"ScrollToPlugin is GSAP's canonical answer for animated `window.scrollY` tweens — it integrates with the rest of the GSAP timeline system (so future work like a ScrollTrigger-driven progress bar shares the same scroller), respects `gsap.ticker`, and gives a real ease curve instead of the browser-defined `scroll-behavior: smooth` (which the GSAP docs explicitly warn conflicts with the plugin — keep `scroll-behavior: auto` on `html`/`body`). `power2.inOut` over 1.2s is the GSAP-recommended sweet spot for long-distance auto-scroll: long enough that the reader feels the page travel (orienting them in the chapter) but short enough to never feel sluggish. `autoKill: true` is the load-bearing safety bit — the reader can wheel/touch at any time and the tween yields, so a stale or wrong saved position can never trap them. (Note: GSAP forum threads flag that wheel-based autoKill is rock-solid in Edge/Safari and Firefox, but Chrome historically under-fires on trackpad wheel events. If that becomes a problem, add a one-shot `wheel`/`touchstart` listener that calls `gsap.killTweensOf(window)` as a belt-and-braces cancel — but for a 1.2s tween the default autoKill is usually enough.) Routing the call through the project's existing `@/lib/gsap` indirection keeps the SSR guard centralised; routing it through `useGSAP` gives automatic context cleanup on Next.js route changes, so a back-button mid-tween won't leave a dangling animation on the next page.","recommendedDemo":"https://gsap.com/docs/v3/Plugins/ScrollToPlugin/ — the official docs page demos `gsap.to(window, { duration: 2, scrollTo: { y: 400, autoKill: true } })` against a long scrolling document, which is exactly the chapter-page shape.","a11yNotes":"Three hard requirements, all already encoded in the sketch above: (1) `prefersReducedMotion()` short-circuits the tween and performs a synchronous `window.scrollTo(0, y)` — no animation, no vestibular trigger; the reader still lands at their saved spot. (2) `autoKill: true` plus a low-effort cancel-on-input means a reader who starts scrolling — including via keyboard PageDown, screen-reader virtual cursor moves, or assistive-tech focus changes — instantly regains control; the page never "fights back". (3) The hash-precedence checks (`:~:text=` and any other `#anchor`) guarantee that a reader arriving via a shared deep-link is never yanked away from the linked passage by a stale saved position — `TextFragmentScroller` (and the Chromium native fragment directive) own that arrival. Also worth noting: do not set CSS `scroll-behavior: smooth` on `html` while ScrollToPlugin is active — the GSAP docs warn these conflict, and the browser-level smooth-scroll bypasses `autoKill` so the user-cancel guarantee breaks. Finally, only run the tween for offsets > ~8px so a reader who saved at the top of the chapter doesn't see a no-op animation."}

TAG FILTER REC: {"useCase":"Tag-filter toggles cause a list of story cards to re-order; we want each card to smoothly glide from its old position to its new one rather than snap, so the filter feels like a real spatial rearrangement instead of a hard re-render.","plugin":"GSAP Flip (free in GSAP 3.12+, no Club required)","approach":"Capture layout with `Flip.getState(".story-card", { props: "opacity" })` BEFORE the React state update (in the click handler, while the old DOM is still mounted), then setState to apply the new filter, then in `useLayoutEffect` (or `useGSAP` with the new filter as a dependency) call `Flip.from(state, { duration: 0.6, ease: "power2.inOut", absolute: true, stagger: 0.02, onEnter: fadeIn, onLeave: fadeOut })`. The handler-then-effect split is critical: getState must run synchronously in the event handler while the previous order is still in the DOM — by the time the effect fires React has already committed, so capturing inside the effect would record the new positions and animate nothing. Give every card a stable `data-flip-id={story.id}` so Flip matches the same logical cards across the re-render even though React may swap DOM nodes. `absolute: true` briefly takes cards out of flow during the tween so a CSS grid or flex container does not fight the inverted transforms, and `onEnter` / `onLeave` cover cards filtered in and out. Reduced motion: read `window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches` at the top of the handler and just `setFilter()` without capturing state — React's normal render becomes the fallback. This works cleanly only with client-side filtering of a stable, already-fetched list; if the filter is driven by URL params and a server round-trip (Next.js server components, RSC, `router.push` with `?tag=...`), the cards are new React tree instances post-navigation and the captured state has nothing to flip from, so either keep filtering client-side in a `'use client'` component or use Flip's `Flip.batch()` with a sessionStorage handoff (rare and fragile — client-side is the natural fit).","codeSketch":"```tsx
"use client";
import { useState, useLayoutEffect, useRef } from "react";
import { gsap } from "gsap";
import { Flip } from "gsap/Flip";
gsap.registerPlugin(Flip);

export function StoryGrid({ stories }: { stories: Story[] }) {
  const [tag, setTag] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const flipState = useRef<Flip.FlipState | null>(null);

  const visible = tag ? stories.filter(s => s.tags.includes(tag)) : stories;

  const onTagClick = (next: string | null) => {
    const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
    if (!reduce) {
      // Capture BEFORE setState — old DOM is still mounted right now
      flipState.current = Flip.getState(".story-card", { props: "opacity" });
    }
    setTag(next);
  };

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    if (!flipState.current) return;
    Flip.from(flipState.current, {
      duration: 0.6,
      ease: "power2.inOut",
      absolute: true,            // pin cards during tween — grid won't fight inversions
      stagger: 0.02,
      onEnter: el => gsap.fromTo(el, { opacity: 0, scale: 0.9 }, { opacity: 1, scale: 1, duration: 0.4 }),
      onLeave: el => gsap.to(el, { opacity: 0, scale: 0.9, duration: 0.3 }),
    });
    flipState.current = null;
  }, [tag]);

  return (
    <>
      <TagBar onSelect={onTagClick} />
      <div className="grid grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        {visible.map(s => (
          <article key={s.id} data-flip-id={s.id} className="story-card">{s.title}</article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </>
  );
}
```","rationale":"Flip is GSAP's canonical solution for "DOM rearranged itself, animate the delta" — it implements the FLIP technique (First/Last/Invert/Play) for you, including the inverted transform math and cleanup. The handler-captures-state pattern sidesteps React's biggest Flip gotcha (effects run AFTER commit, too late to measure the old layout). `data-flip-id` decouples Flip's matching from React's DOM identity, so even if React unmounts and remounts cards under a different filter, Flip still recognizes them as the same logical item. `absolute: true` is what makes Flip play nicely with CSS grid — without it, grid auto-placement re-runs every frame and fights the tween.","recommendedDemo":"https://demos.gsap.com/plugin/flip/ — the Flip plugin demo hub. The clearest official reorder reference is "Animate Between Different Targets" (https://demos.gsap.com/demo/animate-between-different-targets/) and the broader Flip CodePen collection at https://codepen.io/collection/qOMVGv. For a React-specific reorder/filter, the rhernandog/gsap-flip-react repo (https://github.com/rhernandog/gsap-flip-react) is the closest 1:1 to this use case, and Codrops' "Animating Responsive Grid Layout Transitions with GSAP Flip" (https://tympanus.net/codrops/2026/01/20/animating-responsive-grid-layout-transitions-with-gsap-flip/) covers the grid-specific edge cases.","a11yNotes":"Respect `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` by skipping `Flip.getState` entirely in the click handler so the effect short-circuits and React's normal commit becomes the fallback (cards just appear in their new positions, no movement). Keep tweens under 600ms so even non-reduced-motion users aren't waiting on the filter. Tag buttons should be real `<button>` elements with `aria-pressed` reflecting the active tag, and the grid container can stay a static landmark — Flip only moves visuals, it doesn't reorder DOM source order beyond what React already does, so screen reader order follows the filtered array naturally."}

