- Notes: {"area":"Admin notes panel — proximity-hover (macOS-dock) target for the row of collapsed note pills inside NotesPanel.","files":["/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/admin/_components/NotesPanel.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/admin/_components/NotesPanel.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/admin/_components/NoteCard.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/admin/_components/NoteCard.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/AGENTS.md"],"components":["NotesPanel (default export, client component) — renders `<aside className={styles.panel}>` containing `<header>`, `<div className={styles.list}>` (the pill rail), and the `+ Add note` button. Owns `notes`, `expandedId`, `mobileOpen`, `busy`, `error`. Mounted from the admin editor (see EditorWithNotes.tsx).","NoteCard (default export, client component) — receives `note`, `expanded`, `onExpand`, `onCollapse`, `onChange`, `onDelete`. Renders one of two outer divs: collapsed `<div className={`${styles.card} ${styles.collapsed}`} data-color={note.color}>` (pill) or expanded variant. Collapsed children: `<button className={styles.pillBody}>` with `<span className={styles.dot}/>` + `<span className={styles.pillTitle}>` and a sibling 3-dot `menu` (`.menuWrap`).","NOTE_COLORS const + DotsGlyph/CheckGlyph/NotesGlyph/CrossGlyph inline SVG helpers (NoteCard.tsx, NotesPanel.tsx)."],"currentBehavior":"The pill list JSX/CSS shape (collapsed state):\n\n  <aside className={styles.panel} [mobileOpen?] [hasExpanded?]>\n    <header className={styles.header}> title + closeMobile </header>\n    <div className={styles.list}>            <-- THIS is the scroll container for the row of pills\n      {error && <p className={styles.error}/>}\n      {loading-empty messages}\n      {notes.map(note => (\n        <NoteCard key={note.id} note expanded={expandedId===note.id} ...>\n          // collapsed branch renders:\n          <div className={`${styles.card} ${styles.collapsed}`} data-color={note.color}>\n            <button className={styles.pillBody} onClick={onExpand}>\n              <span className={styles.dot}/>\n              <span className={styles.pillTitle}>{displayTitle}</span>\n            </button>\n            <div ref={menuRef} className={styles.menuWrap}>… 3-dot menu …</div>\n          </div>\n        </NoteCard>\n      ))}\n    </div>\n    <button className={styles.addBtn}>+ Add note</button>\n  </aside>\n\n.list (NotesPanel.module.css L65–78): `display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:8px; flex:1 1 auto; min-height:0; overflow-y:auto; padding:4px 0;` — vertical stack of pills with a vertical scrollbar. Note: this is a COLUMN flex, so dock-style proximity will be along the Y axis (cursor's vertical distance to each pill's vertical center), not horizontal like a real macOS dock. Worth confirming with the user if they wanted horizontal.\n\nEach collapsed pill (NoteCard.module.css L7–22, L32–49): `.card` sets `transition: border-color, transform 180ms cubic-bezier(0.16,1,0.3,1), box-shadow 220ms cubic-bezier(0.16,1,0.3,1)`. `.collapsed` is a row-flex pill (`flex-direction:row; align-items:center; gap:4px; padding:4px 6px 4px 12px; border-radius:var(--radius-pill)`). On `:hover` it sets `border-color: var(--note-color); transform: translateY(-1px); box-shadow: 0 6px 14px -8px color-mix(... var(--note-color) 55%, transparent)`. On `:active` it resets transform and clears the shadow. There is no `width`/`height`/`scale` declaration on the pill — its size is intrinsic to content (the title text).\n\nThe expanded card uses `animation: noteExpandIn 200ms cubic-bezier(0.16,1,0.3,1) both;` on mount/swap.\n\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) in BOTH modules zeroes transitions/animations, including the pill hover transform + shadow.","integrationPoints":"Attach cursor listeners on the `.list` container in NotesPanel.tsx (the `<div className={styles.list}>` at line 146). That element already owns the pill stack and the scroll, so its bounding rect + scrollTop is the right local coordinate space. Concrete wiring suggestion:\n\n  - Add a `listRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)` in NotesPanel and `<div ref={listRef} className={styles.list} ...>`.\n  - Listen `onPointerMove` / `onPointerLeave` on that div (NOT `mousemove` on window — avoids work when cursor is elsewhere on screen).\n  - Inside the move handler, query `listRef.current.querySelectorAll(':scope > div[data-color]')` (or expose a `data-note-pill` attribute on the collapsed `.card` to avoid grabbing the expanded card when one is open) to get every pill, read each `getBoundingClientRect()`, compute the distance from cursor Y to the pill's center Y, and drive a falloff (e.g. gaussian over ~120–180px) per-pill.\n  - For GSAP: use a `gsap.quickTo()` per pill on `scale` (and optionally `y`) for a per-frame interpolation that matches dock feel; reset on `pointerleave`.\n  - NoteCard collapsed branch should accept either a ref-forwarding outer div or a stable selector (`data-note-pill` attribute) so the parent can enumerate without a child-side re-render. Easiest non-invasive change: spread a `data-note-pill="true"` on the collapsed `<div className={... styles.collapsed}>` (NoteCard.tsx L176–179).\n  - Skip the effect entirely when `expandedId` is set (only one pill in the rail then — nothing to magnify) OR when `window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches`.\n  - The `.list` is `overflow-y:auto` and lives inside a sticky aside whose `top` animates (`hasExpanded`), so use `getBoundingClientRect()` each move rather than caching positions — pill rects shift during scroll and during the 260ms panel growth transition.\n\nOther mount/data context: the panel is rendered by `EditorWithNotes.tsx` and shows up on the admin story/chapter editor. The list of pills is bound to React state (`notes`) and mutates (add/delete/optimistic update), so any cached pill node array on the GSAP side must re-sync on each render — recommend re-querying inside the pointermove handler (cheap, small N) or running a layout effect keyed on `notes.length` to refresh the quickTo map.","notes":"EXISTING TRANSITIONS THAT WILL COLLIDE WITH GSAP — call out before wiring:\n\n1. `.card` (NoteCard.module.css L18–22) already has `transition: transform 180ms ... , box-shadow 220ms ...`. If GSAP writes `transform` every frame on pointermove, the CSS transition will fight it — each GSAP tween will be re-eased by the CSS transition, producing a perceptible lag/smear. Two options: (a) clear the CSS transition for `transform` on the pills while the proximity effect is active (toggle a class on `.list` that sets `transition: border-color var(--transition-fast)` only on its `.collapsed` children), or (b) drive a CSS custom property (e.g. `--dock-scale`) from GSAP and let CSS apply `transform: scale(var(--dock-scale, 1))` — but the existing `:hover` rule writes `transform: translateY(-1px)` which would clobber `scale()` unless rewritten to be additive.\n\n2. `.collapsed:hover` (L39–45) sets `transform: translateY(-1px)` and a colored `box-shadow`. With proximity hover, EVERY pill near the cursor needs the lift, not just the one being `:hover`'d. Either (a) supersede the `:hover` rule (drop the transform + shadow from `:hover`, let GSAP own them entirely and key both off proximity distance), or (b) coordinate — keep `:hover` for the focal pill and have GSAP only animate the neighbours. Cleanest is (a): the dock metaphor IS proximity, so `:hover` becomes redundant.\n\n3. `.collapsed:active` resets transform to `translateY(0) scale(0.995)`. If GSAP owns the transform during the press, the click squish needs to be expressed as a quickTo too, or `:active` should bump CSS specificity via `!important` (last resort).\n\n4. `.expanded` runs a 200ms `noteExpandIn` keyframe animation on swap (L106). When the user clicks a magnified pill, GSAP should release that pill's tween (reset to scale 1) BEFORE the React state swap so the keyframe animation starts from the natural transform, otherwise the expand-in will visually pop.\n\n5. The panel itself transitions `max-height/min-height/top` over 260ms when `hasExpanded` toggles (NotesPanel.module.css L28–32, L38–45). During that 260ms, pill `getBoundingClientRect()` positions change every frame — proximity math must read live rects, not memoized ones.\n\n6. `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` blocks (NoteCard.module.css L333–348, NotesPanel.module.css L242–252) explicitly zero the hover transform/shadow. The GSAP effect must mirror this: bail out of the listener install if reduced-motion is requested, and respond to `matchMedia` change events (writer can toggle this OS-level mid-session).\n\n7. The pill stack is `flex-direction: column` (vertical), so this is a *vertical* proximity dock. Real macOS dock is horizontal. If the user wants horizontal magnification, the `.list` flex direction needs flipping (which conflicts with `overflow-y` scroll behavior) — worth surfacing as a clarification before building.\n\nRecommendation: have GSAP own `scale` (+ optional `y`) and `box-shadow` via JS-set inline styles, REMOVE the existing `.collapsed:hover` `transform`/`box-shadow` rules so there's a single source of truth, keep CSS for `border-color` on hover (cheap, doesn't fight transform), and gate the whole thing behind a reduced-motion check + `expandedId == null` check."}
- Like / Save: {"area":"Reading footer pills — LikeButton and SaveButton in the story/chapter reader.","files":["/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/LikeButton.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/LikeButton.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/SaveButton.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/SaveButton.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/lib/gsap.ts","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/globals.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/story/[id]/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/chapter/[id]/page.tsx"],"components":["LikeButton (default export from components/LikeButton.tsx) — pill button with inline Heart SVG, optimistic like toggle, count display","SaveButton (default export from components/SaveButton.tsx) — pill button with inline BookmarkIcon SVG, optimistic save toggle, label Save/Saved; anonymous users get a Link to /signup instead of a button"],"currentBehavior":"LikeButton already has a GSAP scaffold: imports gsap, prefersReducedMotion, useGSAP from @/lib/gsap, a buttonRef on the <button>, and a useGSAP scope wrapping the ref. It defines a `pulse()` helper that does `gsap.fromTo(buttonRef.current, { scale: 1 }, { scale: 1.15, duration: 0.1, yoyo: true, repeat: 1, ease: 'power2.out' })`, gated on prefersReducedMotion(). `pulse()` is invoked at the top of `toggle()` (the onClick handler) before the optimistic state mutation. CSS-side, the .button has only `transition: color/background/border-color var(--transition-fast)` — no scale/transform transition; the .liked class swaps color/border/background to the --like family. The Heart SVG has its own `transition: fill var(--transition-fast)` on .icon.\n\nSaveButton has NO animation today: no gsap import, no ref, no pulse. The onClick is `() => void toggle()`; `toggle()` flips `saved` optimistically, calls postSave/deleteSave, and rolls back on non-409 errors. CSS-side, .button transitions color/border-color/background only; the .saved class swaps to --gold / --gold-dim. The BookmarkIcon has no transition.\n\nBoth components disable the button while `pending` is true (SaveButton also disables until `hydrated`). The anonymous SaveButton renders an <a> (Next Link) with no click handler at all — animation should be skipped there.","integrationPoints":"Target element for both: the <button ref={buttonRef}> root (the pill). Hook the animation into the existing onClick path — specifically, inside the `toggle` function before the optimistic state mutation (same spot LikeButton already calls `pulse()`).\n\nLikeButton: extend the existing `pulse()` (LikeButton.tsx lines 47-55) — already called on line 59 inside `toggle`. Use `var(--like)` (e.g., #e07b7b) for the click-feedback color (flash/glow/ring). Keep the prefersReducedMotion() guard.\n\nSaveButton: needs the full scaffold added — (1) `import { gsap, prefersReducedMotion, useGSAP } from '@/lib/gsap'`, (2) `const buttonRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null)` and attach to the authenticated <button> on line 97, (3) a useGSAP({ scope: buttonRef }) for cleanup, (4) a `pulse()` mirroring LikeButton's, and (5) call it at the top of `toggle` (before line 74's `const wasSaved = saved`). Use `var(--gold)` (e.g., #bf9b5e) for the click-feedback color. Do NOT animate the anonymous Link variant (lines 58-70) — keep it purely a navigation link.\n\nBoth: respect the existing `disabled` short-circuit (`if (pending) return`) so the pulse only fires when the click will actually run. Read CSS vars at runtime via `getComputedStyle(buttonRef.current).getPropertyValue('--like' | '--gold')` if the animation needs the color value (theme-aware: --like and --gold are redefined for dark and high-contrast themes).","notes":"Per-button colors are already defined as CSS custom properties in app/globals.css and used by the existing active states:\n\n  - LikeButton active color: --like (#e07b7b light / #d97070 dark / #b5483f high-contrast) and --like-tint (rgba alpha bg). Used by .liked in LikeButton.module.css line 40-49 and by .iconFilled (fill+stroke).\n  - SaveButton active color: --gold (#bf9b5e light / #c9a26a dark / #8a6a2e high-contrast) and --gold-dim (rgba alpha bg). Used by .saved in SaveButton.module.css line 36-47.\n\nUse those vars so the click-flash automatically follows the theme; don't hardcode hex. Other shared design tokens in play: --bg-surface, --border-line, --border-line-hover, --text, --text-muted, --radius-pill, --transition-fast, --font-ui, --fs-tag, --tracking-tag.\n\nGSAP is centralized in lib/gsap.ts — always import gsap/useGSAP/prefersReducedMotion from '@/lib/gsap', never directly from 'gsap'. ScrollTrigger is auto-registered there. prefersReducedMotion() should gate any motion (LikeButton already does this; SaveButton must follow the same pattern).\n\nLikeButton is consumed by app/story/[id]/page.tsx and app/chapter/[id]/page.tsx; SaveButton is consumed by the same two pages. Both buttons sit side-by-side in the reading footer pill row alongside Share, so the click feedback should stay subtle/scale-based to keep that row visually consistent.\n\nframework note (per frontend/AGENTS.md): this Next.js may diverge from training data — both components are already "use client" with the correct hook setup, so no app-router server-component pitfalls to worry about here."}
- Share: {"area":"ShareButton + QuoteShare — current click handlers, existing transitions/animations, and where to wire GSAP click-feedback (with an opinion on whether to replace the QuoteShare keyframe pop-in)","files":["/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/ShareButton.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/ShareButton.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/QuoteShare.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/QuoteShare.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/lib/gsap.ts","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/LikeButton.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/chapter/[id]/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/story/[id]/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/series/[id]/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/ReadingView.tsx"],"components":["ShareButton (single pill — Web Share API w/ clipboard fallback + 'Copied' confirmation)","QuoteShare (selection-anchored popover toolbar — X / Facebook / Bluesky / Copy quote / Copy link, with per-button 'Copied' flash)","LikeButton (reference: sibling pill that already does GSAP scale-pulse on click — same row, share button should match its language)","lib/gsap.ts (shared GSAP entry — exports gsap, useGSAP, prefersReducedMotion; plugins registered once, SSR-safe)"],"currentBehavior":"ShareButton (components/ShareButton.tsx)\n- Single onClick handler `() => void onShare()` on the button (line 58). `onShare` (lines 31-53): tries `navigator.share`, falls back to `navigator.clipboard.writeText(href)`, then flips `copied` state for 1800ms (visible-label swap 'Share' -> 'Copied'). No motion — only CSS color/border/background transitions via `var(--transition-fast)` on `.button` (CSS lines 16-20). No ref on the button yet; would need a `useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null)` to hook GSAP.\n\nQuoteShare (components/QuoteShare.tsx)\n- Each toolbar pill renders with `onClick={() => void onShareClick(target, i)}` (line 217). `onShareClick` (lines 170-185) either `window.open(target.action.url, ...)` for URL share targets (X / Facebook / Bluesky) or `navigator.clipboard.writeText` for copy/link targets — successful copy sets `copiedIndex=i` and the matching button gets `styles.buttonCopied` (gold flash) for 1200ms with the glyph swapping to the `CheckGlyph`.\n- Popover container has an existing CSS keyframe entrance: `.popover { animation: quoteShareIn 140ms ease-out; }` defined in QuoteShare.module.css lines 21-33 — opacity 0->1 + translateY(2px)->0 + scale(0.98)->1. Added in PR #181.\n- There is also a separate `.fragmentFlash` keyframe for the amber highlight when arriving via a text-fragment deep-link (CSS lines 100-117) — unrelated to click feedback but worth noting since QuoteShare owns it.\n- No button refs today; the only ref on the component is `popoverRef` (the container div). A click-feedback hook needs per-button refs OR an event-target-driven `gsap.fromTo(e.currentTarget, ...)`.\n\nReference pattern already in the codebase — components/LikeButton.tsx lines 39-55:\n```\nconst buttonRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);\nuseGSAP(() => {}, { scope: buttonRef });\nconst pulse = () => {\n  if (!buttonRef.current) return;\n  if (prefersReducedMotion()) return;\n  gsap.fromTo(buttonRef.current, { scale: 1 },\n    { scale: 1.15, duration: 0.1, yoyo: true, repeat: 1, ease: 'power2.out' });\n};\n```\n`pulse()` is invoked at the very top of the click handler (line 59) — synchronously, before the optimistic state flip and the awaited network call.","integrationPoints":"Where the GSAP click feedback should hook in:\n\n1) ShareButton.tsx\n   - Add `const buttonRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);` and attach it via `ref={buttonRef}` to the `<button>` at line 56.\n   - Import from `@/lib/gsap`: `gsap`, `useGSAP`, `prefersReducedMotion` (never from raw `gsap` — see lib/gsap.ts header comment).\n   - Add a `useGSAP(() => {}, { scope: buttonRef })` scope for auto-cleanup, matching LikeButton.\n   - Define a local `pulse()` mirroring LikeButton's signature (scale 1 -> 1.15, 0.1s, yoyo, power2.out) so the three pills (Like / Save / Share) read with one consistent language. Call it at the top of `onShare` (before the navigator.share / clipboard branch).\n   - Optional refinement: a separate, slightly distinct confirmation flash when the clipboard fallback fires and `setCopied(true)` resolves — e.g. a quick color/scale tween on the icon — but for parity with Like the single scale-pulse on every click is the minimum.\n\n2) QuoteShare.tsx\n   - The simplest hook is `onShareClick(target, i, e)` taking the click event and using `e.currentTarget` for the tween target — no per-button ref array needed, and it keeps the toolbar a flat `map`. Call `gsap.fromTo(e.currentTarget, { scale: 1 }, { scale: 1.12, duration: 0.1, yoyo: true, repeat: 1, ease: 'power2.out' })` at the very top of the handler (guarded by `prefersReducedMotion()`).\n   - Update the JSX to `onClick={(e) => void onShareClick(target, i, e)}` at line 217. No scope/useGSAP wrapper is strictly necessary for a one-shot fromTo, but adding `useGSAP(() => {}, { scope: popoverRef })` is consistent with the rest of the codebase and gives cleanup on unmount.\n   - Brand-glyph buttons (X / Facebook / Bluesky) close the popover immediately via `dismiss()` after `window.open`, so the tween will be cut off when the popover unmounts — that's fine, the user has already left for the new tab. For Copy / Copy-link the popover persists 1200ms, so the pulse plays in full alongside the existing `.buttonCopied` gold flash and glyph-swap to `CheckGlyph` — they layer well (color transition is CSS, scale is GSAP).\n\nFiles a follow-up PR will touch: ShareButton.tsx and QuoteShare.tsx (handlers + ref/useGSAP wiring). No CSS changes required for the click pulse since it's pure transform. lib/gsap.ts is already the canonical import surface.","notes":"On whether to also replace the popover pop-in (CSS `@keyframes quoteShareIn`) with a GSAP entrance:\n\nRecommendation: leave the keyframe alone in this change, OR replace it cleanly — do not stack both.\n\nReasons to leave it as-is\n- It's brand new (PR #181), short (140ms), and tied to mount; CSS keyframes are the right tool for one-shot enter animations that don't need imperative control. The site uses CSS animations elsewhere for similar enter flashes (e.g. `.fragmentFlash` in the same file).\n- Mixing a CSS `animation` on `.popover` with a GSAP tween on the same element (or its descendants) at mount risks transform-origin / will-change conflicts and clobbered transforms when GSAP also targets `transform: scale()` on buttons.\n- The user asked specifically for click feedback on the share affordance — the popover entrance is a separate concern.\n\nReasons to convert (only if doing it)\n- GSAP would give you `prefersReducedMotion()` short-circuiting in one place rather than relying on the browser's CSS-level reduced-motion handling (the current keyframe doesn't honor it — worth checking).\n- An imperative `useGSAP` entrance lets the popover also play an exit tween before unmount (currently it just disappears), which is the bigger UX win than the entrance itself.\n- It would unify motion idioms across the codebase — every other animated component (CardGrid, ReadingView, ExpandableSection, SiteHeader, BlockedTagsButton, ReadingPrefs, LikeButton, admin layout) already uses `useGSAP`.\n\nIf the choice is to convert: delete `animation: quoteShareIn ...` + the `@keyframes quoteShareIn` block from QuoteShare.module.css, add a `useGSAP(() => { gsap.from(popoverRef.current, { opacity: 0, y: 2, scale: 0.98, duration: 0.14, ease: 'power2.out' }) }, { scope: popoverRef, dependencies: [selection?.rect] })` so it re-plays each time a new selection mounts the popover. Then to add the exit animation, refactor mount/unmount to keep the element rendered behind an `isOpen` boolean and tween `opacity/y/scale` back to the from-state before clearing `selection`.\n\nMy suggestion for this task: ship the GSAP click pulse on both components first (small, isolated, matches LikeButton). If desired, follow up with a dedicated PR that converts the popover entrance to GSAP and adds an exit tween at the same time — that's a more substantive UX change and should not be hidden inside a 'click feedback' commit.\n\nMisc notes\n- ShareButton appears in three pages: app/chapter/[id]/page.tsx (line 225), app/story/[id]/page.tsx (line 174), app/series/[id]/page.tsx (line 158). The change is local to the component — no caller updates needed.\n- QuoteShare is mounted once from components/ReadingView.tsx (line 238); also local.\n- app/globals.css line 613 has a `[class*='ShareButton']` selector — worth a glance before changing the module-scoped class names, but adding a ref/handler doesn't affect that.\n- Always import GSAP via `@/lib/gsap` (per the lib's own header comment) — never from `gsap` directly.\n- `useGSAP` from `@gsap/react` is re-exported from lib/gsap.ts and is the standard scope wrapper across the codebase."}
- Scroll restore: {"area":"Reading-position scroll restore (GSAP-driven smooth resume)","files":["/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/lib/useReadingPosition.ts","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/ReadingView.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/TextFragmentScroller.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/lib/gsap.ts","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/lib/api.ts","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/chapter/[id]/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/story/[id]/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/QuoteShare.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/package.json"],"components":["useReadingPosition (lib/useReadingPosition.ts) — fetch-stored-position + throttled-write-on-scroll + sendBeacon-on-hide hook. Restore is the relevant half: it computes `target = (pos.scrollPercentage / 100) * (doc.scrollHeight - innerHeight)` and calls `window.scrollTo({ top: target, behavior: 'instant' })` inside `requestIdleCallback(restore, { timeout: 500 })` (falls back to a single rAF on Safari). Restore is gated by `enabled`, by `pos.pageNumber === pageNumber`, and by `pos.scrollPercentage >= RESTORE_MIN_PERCENT (4)`.","ReadingView (components/ReadingView.tsx) — sole consumer of useReadingPosition. Calls the hook only in paged mode: `pageNumber: mode === 'paged' ? currentPage : null, enabled: mode === 'paged'`. Already imports `gsap, prefersReducedMotion, useGSAP` from @/lib/gsap; runs an opacity/y fade-in on `[mode, currentPage]` via useGSAP scoped to `contentRef`. Page swaps and mode switches use `window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'auto' })`. Scroll-to-top button uses native `behavior: 'smooth'`.","TextFragmentScroller (components/TextFragmentScroller.tsx) — Firefox/Safari `#:~:text=...` polyfill. Mounted as a sibling of ReadingView in both `app/chapter/[id]/page.tsx` (line 152) and `app/story/[id]/page.tsx` (line 110). Bails out when `'fragmentDirective' in document` (Chromium). Defers via DOUBLE rAF, then calls `target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'center' })` plus a 2.4s `.fragmentFlash` class on the nearest block. Returns null.","lib/gsap.ts — central GSAP entrypoint. Currently registers ONLY ScrollTrigger; `ScrollToPlugin.js` is present in node_modules/gsap but not registered. Also exports `prefersReducedMotion()` and re-exports `useGSAP` from @gsap/react.","lib/api.ts — `getReadingPosition(contentType, contentId, sessionToken)` returns `{ scrollPercentage, pageNumber } | null` from `/api/reading-positions`."],"currentBehavior":""Current restore is INSTANT, not animated. Sequence: (1) on mount/contentId/pageNumber change, useReadingPosition fetches the stored row; (2) if the page matches and percentage >= 4, it schedules `restore` via `requestIdleCallback(..., { timeout: 500 })` (single rAF fallback on Safari); (3) `restore()` computes `target = (pct/100) * (scrollHeight - innerHeight)` and calls `window.scrollTo({ top: target, behavior: 'instant' })`. There is no tween, no easing, no GSAP involvement — `behavior: 'instant'` is the explicit choice. The reader jumps. Restore is also page-aware (paged mode only) and is silently skipped on network failure, percentage < 4, or page mismatch. Concurrent reader writes are throttled at 5s with a trailing flush and a final beacon on visibilitychange/pagehide/unmount."","integrationPoints":""Single insertion point: replace the `window.scrollTo({ top: target, behavior: 'instant' })` call inside the `restore` closure in lib/useReadingPosition.ts (~line 119) with a GSAP tween:\n\n  gsap.to(window, {\n    duration: 1.1, // or scale by distance: gsap.utils.clamp(0.6, 1.6, target / 1800)\n    scrollTo: { y: target, autoKill: true }, // autoKill aborts if the user scrolls/wheels mid-tween\n    ease: 'power2.out',\n    overwrite: 'auto',\n  });\n\nPrereq edits (do these first):\n  1. lib/gsap.ts — import and register ScrollToPlugin alongside ScrollTrigger inside the `typeof window !== 'undefined'` guard, and re-export it. The plugin file already exists at node_modules/gsap/ScrollToPlugin.js. Without this, the tween silently no-ops.\n  2. lib/useReadingPosition.ts — import `{ gsap, prefersReducedMotion }` from '@/lib/gsap'; keep the existing rIC/rAF deferral and the `target <= 0` / `!Number.isFinite(target)` guards. If `prefersReducedMotion()` returns true, fall back to the current instant `window.scrollTo`. Consider also bailing to instant when `target > scrollHeight - innerHeight` after content shift (clamp via `gsap.utils.clamp(0, doc.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight, target)`).\n  3. Keep the existing rIC/rAF wrapper — it's what gets us past hydration so `scrollHeight` is real. Tweening before that point would tween to 0.\n\nDo NOT extend TextFragmentScroller. Reasons:\n  - It runs only when the URL hash contains `:~:text=`, so 100% of normal returning-reader sessions would bypass it.\n  - It owns a different concern (DOM text walk + Range + block flash) on a different gating signal (hash) with a different target unit (an element, not a Y offset).\n  - The two paths can co-occur on a single visit (returning reader who arrived via a shared `#:~:text=` link). The right precedence is: text-fragment wins — it represents an explicit user intent (the share link) over a stored heuristic. Make ScrollRestore defer when `window.location.hash.includes(':~:text=')` OR when `document.fragmentDirective` is going to act. Simplest implementation: inside useReadingPosition's restore() add an early `if (window.location.hash.includes(':~:text=')) return;` so the polyfill's smooth scrollIntoView (or Chromium's native one) runs unopposed. TextFragmentScroller already uses double rAF (post-hydration), useReadingPosition uses rIC; with the early bail there is no race.\n\nDo NOT factor this into a separate ScrollRestore React component. The trigger lives entirely inside useReadingPosition's existing fetch-then-restore flow — a new component would just re-do the API call or need a prop bridge. Keep it as a one-line swap inside the hook.\n\nOther call sites to leave alone: ReadingView's `goToPage` and `switchToPaged` use `behavior: 'auto'` deliberately (page change = jump to top); the scroll-to-top button uses native smooth scroll. Animating those via GSAP is out of scope for this task."","notes":""Edge cases worth handling in the swap: (1) if the reader has already manually scrolled before rIC fires (e.g. fast-paint Safari + impatient user), `autoKill: true` plus `overwrite: 'auto'` lets us tween without yanking them — but you may also want a `Math.abs(window.scrollY - 0) > 50 ? bail : tween` guard before kicking off; (2) mode toggle: useReadingPosition is intentionally `enabled: false` in infinite mode, so the tween only runs in paged mode — matches today's behavior; (3) `restore()` closes over `cancelled`; the unmount cleanup sets it true. With a 1.1s tween in flight, kill it explicitly via `const tween = gsap.to(window, {...}); ` and call `tween.kill()` in the cleanup so a route-change during the animation doesn't keep scrolling the next page; (4) the existing `?page=N` handling in ReadingView means the hook re-fires after a page swap — make sure the tween from a stale page is killed before the new one kicks off (overwrite:'auto' covers this for back-to-back tweens on the same target).""}
- Tag filter: {"area":"Tag filtering UI — TagBar, browse/search/tag pages, BlockedTagsButton, admin TagPicker","files":["/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/TagBar.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/TagBar.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/CardGrid.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/CardGrid.module.css","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/StoryCard.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/SeriesCard.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/BlockedTagsButton.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/components/SiteHeader.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/browse/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/search/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/tag/[slug]/page.tsx","/Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/app/admin/_components/TagPicker.tsx"],"components":["TagBar (default export, components/TagBar.tsx) — single-line horizontal-scroll pill row, plus an expanded letter-grouped mode (A/B/C/…), with safe/adult color variants and a gold-stroke active state","CollapsedRow + Pill + Chevron (internal helpers inside TagBar.tsx)","CardGrid (components/CardGrid.tsx) — grid container the result cards live in, with a GSAP stagger-reveal on first scroll into view","StoryCard / SeriesCard / ChapterCard — the card items that re-render when a tag changes","BrowsePage (app/browse/page.tsx) — uses <TagBar basePath="/search"> as a discovery bar (no activeIds; clicking always navigates to /search?tagIds=…)","SearchPage (app/search/page.tsx) — async server component, reads searchParams.tagIds, refetches getStories/getSeriesList, renders <CardGrid> with StoryCard/SeriesCard","TagPage (app/tag/[slug]/page.tsx) — single-tag landing; resolves slug -> tag.id then renders the same CardGrid layout. Notably does NOT render a TagBar — selecting another tag means navigating back through /search","BlockedTagsButton (components/BlockedTagsButton.tsx) — header trigger panel for per-device tag blocklist; client-side, in-panel search filter + memoised sortedTags reorder (blocked rows pinned top, then alpha)","TagPicker (app/admin/_components/TagPicker.tsx) — author-form picker; selected pills pinned top, unselected pool sorted adult-first then alpha, filtered by a search input"],"currentBehavior":""Filtering is server-driven via URL params. TagBar is a 'use client' component but it only renders next/link <Link> elements — every pill href is built by buildTagHref(id), which writes ?search=…&tagIds=<id> against basePath (default /search), and the 'All' pill href clears tagIds. There is no client filter state and no router.push: clicking a tag is a Next.js App Router navigation. SearchPage and TagPage are async Server Components that read searchParams (or the route slug), call getStories / getSeriesList with the new tagIds, and render the next CardGrid tree. The result cards (StoryCard / SeriesCard inside <CardGrid>) are wholly re-rendered each navigation — they're brand-new DOM nodes with new React keys (story.id / series.id). CardGrid's only animation today is a one-shot GSAP scrollTrigger fade-up stagger that fires once on mount (`once: true`), so subsequent filter changes don't animate the cards at all. The only places filter toggles ARE client-side and could host Flip cheaply: (1) BlockedTagsButton — useMemo sortedTags reorders the in-panel <ul> when blocked toggles or filter text changes, all in the same component tree; (2) TagPicker in admin — useMemo {selected, results} similarly reorders pills when toggle() flips a value or query changes. TagBar's own active-pill restyle does not reorder pills (sortedTags is purely alphabetical, independent of activeSet)."","integrationPoints":""For the primary tag-filter reorder (browse/search/tag): every filter click triggers an App Router server navigation, so the cards being reordered come from different React renders / different DOM nodes. Hook points where a FLIP-style transition could be wired in: (1) TagBar.tsx — replace the next/link <Link> on each Pill with a router.push using useRouter + useSearchParams, then drive the reorder client-side OR keep <Link> and intercept onClick with e.preventDefault before the navigation to record First positions; pair with a useGSAP/useLayoutEffect on CardGrid keyed by serialised tagIds to read Last positions and tween the deltas. (2) CardGrid.tsx — already wraps the card list and has containerRef + useGSAP; this is the natural home for the FLIP read/play loop, and it would need stable card identity exposed (e.g., data-flip-id={story.id || series.id} on StoryCard / SeriesCard root) so the same id can be matched across renders. (3) The two server pages (app/search/page.tsx, app/tag/[slug]/page.tsx) currently pass children directly into <CardGrid>; no API contract change required as long as the cards expose a stable id attribute. (4) For BlockedTagsButton + admin TagPicker the reorder is fully client-side inside one component — Flip can be wired in directly around the useMemo sort with no routing involved. (5) SearchBar at components/SearchBar.tsx already does a router.push, confirming useRouter is the established pattern for query-string updates."","notes":""Yes — server-driven filtering is the central blocker for a vanilla GSAP Flip plugin integration on TagBar/browse/search/tag pages. Flip's contract is read-Before -> mutate-DOM -> read-After -> animate-delta on the SAME DOM nodes (or, with Flip.fit / data-flip-id, on nodes that survived a React reconcile in place). A Next.js App Router navigation re-renders the server tree, swaps the entire <CardGrid> subtree for a fresh React mount, and discards the previous DOM nodes before Flip could ever read 'After'. Three viable workarounds, ordered by intrusiveness: (1) Lightest — let Next.js fetch but intercept the render: in TagBar replace the <Link> with router.push + Flip.getState(cards) called synchronously BEFORE push, then in CardGrid run a useLayoutEffect keyed on the searchParams tagIds (or a serialised version) that calls Flip.from(savedState, …) after the new server render mounts. React preserves DOM nodes that share the same key across renders, so as long as StoryCard/SeriesCard expose data-flip-id={id} and the parent grid is the same DOM container, GSAP can match the surviving ids and animate their position deltas; cards that appeared/disappeared get Flip's onEnter/onLeave handlers. The 'savedState' has to live somewhere that survives the navigation — a module-level Map keyed by a session-scoped token, sessionStorage, or a React context that the new server render's first useLayoutEffect drains. (2) Move filtering client-side: convert SearchPage from a Server Component to a small server shell that fetches a superset of cards once and a client component that filters locally on tagIds in URL or local state. Flip then runs in a single component with all DOM intact. Drops SSR result counts and breaks the /tag/[slug] SEO landing pages — significant. (3) Hybrid: keep server filtering for deep links / SEO but optimistically perform the new filter client-side from a cached over-fetched dataset, then reconcile with the server payload on arrival. Most work, smoothest UX. For the two purely client-side filter surfaces — BlockedTagsButton's tag list and admin TagPicker — Flip drops in cleanly with no routing surgery: wrap the useMemo sort with Flip.getState before the state change and Flip.from after, keyed on tag.id. Those are good prototypes before committing to option (1) for the main browse path. Also worth noting: CardGrid's current GSAP stagger uses `once: true`, so it would need to be guarded to not fight a Flip tween on re-render.""}

